# A COMPILER ICE, not a diagnostic: an `Option[Callable[T]]` nested inside a
# `Result` payload has no registered drop fn, and the LIR's drop-completeness
# check panics.
#
#   thread 'gg-main' panicked at src/lir/validate.rs:137:
#     in @: Drop completeness: enum "Result__Option__Callable__GorgetClosure__GorgetString"
#     has droppable variant payload "Ok_0" (type "Option__Callable__GorgetClosure")
#     missing from type_drop_fns enum_variants — scope-exit drop will leak the payload
#
# ⚠ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS `Callable`, NOT NESTING. `Result[Option[String], String]`
# with the same shape builds and runs (`Ok(Some("hi"))` prints `hi`), and the
# one-level `Result[Callable[T], String]` and `Option[Callable[T]]` both build
# and run. Only the `Option[Callable]`-inside-`Result` combination has no
# `type_drop_fns` entry.
#
# ⚠ NOT the closure-materialization class: it panics at HEAD and with the
# materialization fix landed, with the SAME message, and it never reaches
# codegen at all. The write site is drop-fn REGISTRATION for a nested
# `Callable`-carrying payload — the same family as `todo/t0948`'s
# `field_is_transitively_droppable`.
#
# ⚠⚠ THE TWO COMPILER PROFILES DISAGREE, SO CHECK WHICH ONE YOU RAN.
# `assert_module_valid` runs the LIR validators unconditionally under
# `cfg!(debug_assertions)` and is a NO-OP in release. A DEBUG `gg` ICEs; a
# RELEASE `gg` builds this and prints the correct `2` — while LEAKING the
# payload the validator named (8 bytes in 1 allocation, `__gorget_closure_env_alloc`,
# under `--sanitize`). So the validator is RIGHT and both halves are owed:
# the ICE on legal source, and the missing drop fn behind it.
#
# INTENDED: prints 2, ASan-clean, on both backends and both compiler profiles.
void main():
    Result[Option[Callable[int(int)]], String] r = Ok(Some((int x): x + 1))
    match r:
        case Ok(inner):
            match inner:
                case Some(g):
                    print(g(1))
                else:
                    print(0)
        case Error(e):
            print(e)
